{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>וַיסב.</b> From the doubled root סבב; it also comes in another pattern: וַיָסֶךְ בדלתים (Job 38:5), וַיָגֶל את האבן (Genesis 29:10). Do not listen to those who say its root is נסב." "And the word המדבר draws itself and another with it, namely 'the way of the wilderness,' meaning the wilderness of the Sea of Suph. Similarly: הארון הברית (Joshua 3:14) — the ark, the ark of the covenant; עץ הדעת (Genesis 2:9) — knowledge of good and evil; הספר המקנה (Jeremiah 32:12); היין החמה (Jeremiah 25:15); and many like these." "And Suph is a place name. Some say it derives from סוף דבר (Ecclesiastes 12:13), for it is the end of the world, and it is the Ocean Sea, as I have already mentioned. This is a great error, for it is the eastern sea opposite Egypt, and the sea of Spain is larger than it." "<b>וחמושים.</b> Some say: fully supplied, that they have all their needs. But it is written וגם צדה לא עשו להם (above, chapter 12). What reason to mention this now? Rather, its meaning is: girded with weapons for war, like חלוצים תעברו (Deuteronomy 3:18), which means girded with weapons. The reliable witness: ואתם תעברו חמושים (Joshua 1:14), and in another place he called them חלוצים — for what reason to carry provisions before their brothers? And the reason Scripture mentions וחמושים here is that above it is written בראותם מלחמה: for with raised hand they went out with weapons of war, and not like slaves fleeing."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:34:17.446717755Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}